{"records":[{"id":"66ms37y66x","title":"Data for: A genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screen reveals that riboflavin regulates hydrogen peroxide entry into HAP1 cells  ","description":"CRISPR screen data for hydrogen peroxide mediated cell death","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-13T14:30:16.414Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/66ms37y66x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/66ms37y66x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"surachai","last_name":"supattapone","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kenneth","last_name":"Mark","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tamutenda","last_name":"Chidawanyika","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Oxidative Stress"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mhcf3cz9v7","title":"Metcalf ISCIENCE-D-23-07458R1)_AGD2348_forward","description":"Glia are the protectors of the nervous system, providing neurons with support and protection from cytotoxic insults. We previously discovered that four astrocyte-like glia can regulate organismal proteostasis and longevity in C. elegans. Expression of the UPRER transcription factor, XBP-1s, in these glia increases stress resistance, longevity, and activates the UPRER in intestinal cells via neuropeptides. Autophagy, a key regulator of metabolism and aging, has been described as a cell autonomous process. Surprisingly, we find that glial XBP-1s enhances proteostasis and longevity by cell non-autonomously reprogramming organismal lipid metabolism and activating autophagy. Glial XBP-1s regulates the activation of another transcription factor, HLH-30/TFEB, in the intestine. HLH-30 activates intestinal autophagy, increases intestinal lipid catabolism, and upregulates a robust transcriptional program. Our study reveals a novel role for glia in regulating peripheral lipid metabolism, autophagy, and organellar health through peripheral activation of HLH-30 and autophagy","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-10T06:17:34.19Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mhcf3cz9v7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mhcf3cz9v7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Melissa","last_name":"Metcalf","orcid":"0000-0003-0515-5784"},{"first_name":"Jenni","last_name":"Durieux","orcid":"0000-0002-0016-7332"},{"first_name":"Larry","last_name":"Joe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrew ","last_name":"Dillin","orcid":"0000-0002-7427-2629"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0187kwz08","name":"National Institute for Health Research","grant_id":"R01AG059566"}],"categories":["Glia","Aging","Transcription Factor","Autophagy","Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"p4y4mfb5nc","title":"Metcalf ISCIENCE-D-23-07458R1_AGD2491_forward","description":"Glia are the protectors of the nervous system, providing neurons with support and protection from cytotoxic insults. We previously discovered that four astrocyte-like glia can regulate organismal proteostasis and longevity in C. elegans. Expression of the UPRER transcription factor, XBP-1s, in these glia increases stress resistance, longevity, and activates the UPRER in intestinal cells via neuropeptides. Autophagy, a key regulator of metabolism and aging, has been described as a cell autonomous process. Surprisingly, we find that glial XBP-1s enhances proteostasis and longevity by cell non-autonomously reprogramming organismal lipid metabolism and activating autophagy. Glial XBP-1s regulates the activation of another transcription factor, HLH-30/TFEB, in the intestine. HLH-30 activates intestinal autophagy, increases intestinal lipid catabolism, and upregulates a robust transcriptional program. Our study reveals a novel role for glia in regulating peripheral lipid metabolism, autophagy, and organellar health through peripheral activation of HLH-30 and autophagy","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-10T06:25:03.244Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/p4y4mfb5nc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/p4y4mfb5nc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Melissa","last_name":"Metcalf","orcid":"0000-0003-0515-5784"},{"first_name":"Jenni","last_name":"Durieux","orcid":"0000-0002-0016-7332"},{"first_name":"Larry","last_name":"Joe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrew ","last_name":"Dillin","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0187kwz08","name":"National Institute for Health Research","grant_id":"R01AG059566"}],"categories":["Glia","Aging","Transcription Factor","Autophagy","Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k9r228m6jp","title":"Metcalf ISCIENCE-D-23-07458R1_N2_reverse","description":"Glia are the protectors of the nervous system, providing neurons with support and protection from cytotoxic insults. We previously discovered that four astrocyte-like glia can regulate organismal proteostasis and longevity in C. elegans. Expression of the UPRER transcription factor, XBP-1s, in these glia increases stress resistance, longevity, and activates the UPRER in intestinal cells via neuropeptides. Autophagy, a key regulator of metabolism and aging, has been described as a cell autonomous process. Surprisingly, we find that glial XBP-1s enhances proteostasis and longevity by cell non-autonomously reprogramming organismal lipid metabolism and activating autophagy. Glial XBP-1s regulates the activation of another transcription factor, HLH-30/TFEB, in the intestine. HLH-30 activates intestinal autophagy, increases intestinal lipid catabolism, and upregulates a robust transcriptional program. Our study reveals a novel role for glia in regulating peripheral lipid metabolism, autophagy, and organellar health through peripheral activation of HLH-30 and autophagy","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-10T06:21:37.188Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k9r228m6jp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k9r228m6jp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Melissa","last_name":"Metcalf","orcid":"0000-0003-0515-5784"},{"first_name":"Jenni","last_name":"Durieux","orcid":"0000-0002-0016-7332"},{"first_name":"Larry","last_name":"Joe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Dillin","orcid":"0000-0002-7427-2629"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0187kwz08","name":"National Institute for Health Research","grant_id":"R01AG059566"}],"categories":["Glia","Aging","Transcription Factor","Autophagy","Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"52dwhgfbjg","title":"Metcalf ISCIENCE-D-23-07458R1_AGD1724_forward","description":"Glia are the protectors of the nervous system, providing neurons with support and protection from cytotoxic insults. We previously discovered that four astrocyte-like glia can regulate organismal proteostasis and longevity in C. elegans. Expression of the UPRER transcription factor, XBP-1s, in these glia increases stress resistance, longevity, and activates the UPRER in intestinal cells via neuropeptides. Autophagy, a key regulator of metabolism and aging, has been described as a cell autonomous process. Surprisingly, we find that glial XBP-1s enhances proteostasis and longevity by cell non-autonomously reprogramming organismal lipid metabolism and activating autophagy. Glial XBP-1s regulates the activation of another transcription factor, HLH-30/TFEB, in the intestine. HLH-30 activates intestinal autophagy, increases intestinal lipid catabolism, and upregulates a robust transcriptional program. Our study reveals a novel role for glia in regulating peripheral lipid metabolism, autophagy, and organellar health through peripheral activation of HLH-30 and autophagy”\t\t\t\t\t","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-10T06:22:44.762Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/52dwhgfbjg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/52dwhgfbjg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Melissa ","last_name":"Metcalf","orcid":"0000-0003-0515-5784"},{"first_name":"Jenni","last_name":"Durieux","orcid":"0000-0002-0016-7332"},{"first_name":"Larry","last_name":"Joe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Dillin","orcid":"0000-0002-7427-2629"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0187kwz08","name":"National Institute for Health Research","grant_id":"R01AG059566"}],"categories":["Glia","Aging","Transcription Factor","Autophagy","Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cshv6rjzd9","title":"Metcalf ISCIENCE-D-23-07458R1_N2_forward","description":"Glia are the protectors of the nervous system, providing neurons with support and protection from cytotoxic insults. We previously discovered that four astrocyte-like glia can regulate organismal proteostasis and longevity in C. elegans. Expression of the UPRER transcription factor, XBP-1s, in these glia increases stress resistance, longevity, and activates the UPRER in intestinal cells via neuropeptides. Autophagy, a key regulator of metabolism and aging, has been described as a cell autonomous process. Surprisingly, we find that glial XBP-1s enhances proteostasis and longevity by cell non-autonomously reprogramming organismal lipid metabolism and activating autophagy. Glial XBP-1s regulates the activation of another transcription factor, HLH-30/TFEB, in the intestine. HLH-30 activates intestinal autophagy, increases intestinal lipid catabolism, and upregulates a robust transcriptional program. Our study reveals a novel role for glia in regulating peripheral lipid metabolism, autophagy, and organellar health through peripheral activation of HLH-30 and autophagy","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-10T06:19:31.766Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cshv6rjzd9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cshv6rjzd9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Melissa ","last_name":"Metcalf","orcid":"0000-0003-0515-5784"},{"first_name":"Jenni","last_name":"Durieux","orcid":"0000-0002-0016-7332"},{"first_name":"Larry","last_name":"Joe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Dillin","orcid":"0000-0002-7427-2629"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0187kwz08","name":"National Institute for Health Research","grant_id":"R01AG059566"}],"categories":["Glia","Aging","Transcription Factor","Autophagy","Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yztws2dh4h","title":"Metcalf ISCIENCE-D-23-07458R1_AGD2348_Reverse","description":"Glia are the protectors of the nervous system, providing neurons with support and protection from cytotoxic insults. We previously discovered that four astrocyte-like glia can regulate organismal proteostasis and longevity in C. elegans. Expression of the UPRER transcription factor, XBP-1s, in these glia increases stress resistance, longevity, and activates the UPRER in intestinal cells via neuropeptides. Autophagy, a key regulator of metabolism and aging, has been described as a cell autonomous process. Surprisingly, we find that glial XBP-1s enhances proteostasis and longevity by cell non-autonomously reprogramming organismal lipid metabolism and activating autophagy. Glial XBP-1s regulates the activation of another transcription factor, HLH-30/TFEB, in the intestine. HLH-30 activates intestinal autophagy, increases intestinal lipid catabolism, and upregulates a robust transcriptional program. Our study reveals a novel role for glia in regulating peripheral lipid metabolism, autophagy, and organellar health through peripheral activation of HLH-30 and autophagy","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-10T06:15:57.181Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yztws2dh4h","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yztws2dh4h"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Melissa","last_name":"Metcalf","orcid":"0000-0003-0515-5784"},{"first_name":"Jenni","last_name":"Durieux","orcid":"0000-0002-0016-7332"},{"first_name":"Larry","last_name":"Joe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Dillin","orcid":"0000-0002-7427-2629"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0187kwz08","name":"National Institute for Health Research","grant_id":"R01AG059566"}],"categories":["Glia","Aging","Transcription Factor","Autophagy","Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gn5dtvwwt7","title":"Metcalf ISCIENCE-D-23-07458R1_AGD2491_Reverse","description":"Glia are the protectors of the nervous system, providing neurons with support and protection from cytotoxic insults. We previously discovered that four astrocyte-like glia can regulate organismal proteostasis and longevity in C. elegans. Expression of the UPRER transcription factor, XBP-1s, in these glia increases stress resistance, longevity, and activates the UPRER in intestinal cells via neuropeptides. Autophagy, a key regulator of metabolism and aging, has been described as a cell autonomous process. Surprisingly, we find that glial XBP-1s enhances proteostasis and longevity by cell non-autonomously reprogramming organismal lipid metabolism and activating autophagy. Glial XBP-1s regulates the activation of another transcription factor, HLH-30/TFEB, in the intestine. HLH-30 activates intestinal autophagy, increases intestinal lipid catabolism, and upregulates a robust transcriptional program. Our study reveals a novel role for glia in regulating peripheral lipid metabolism, autophagy, and organellar health through peripheral activation of HLH-30 and autophagy","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-10T06:28:07.837Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gn5dtvwwt7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gn5dtvwwt7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Melissa","last_name":"Metcalf","orcid":"0000-0003-0515-5784"},{"first_name":"Jenni","last_name":"Durieux","orcid":"0000-0002-0016-7332"},{"first_name":"Larry","last_name":"Joe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Dillin","orcid":"0000-0002-7427-2629"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0187kwz08","name":"National Institute for Health Research","grant_id":"R01AG059566"}],"categories":["Glia","Aging","Transcription Factor","Autophagy","Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tww9s66w69","title":"Metcalf ISCIENCE-D-23-07458R1_AGD1724_reverse","description":"Glia are the protectors of the nervous system, providing neurons with support and protection from cytotoxic insults. We previously discovered that four astrocyte-like glia can regulate organismal proteostasis and longevity in C. elegans. Expression of the UPRER transcription factor, XBP-1s, in these glia increases stress resistance, longevity, and activates the UPRER in intestinal cells via neuropeptides. Autophagy, a key regulator of metabolism and aging, has been described as a cell autonomous process. Surprisingly, we find that glial XBP-1s enhances proteostasis and longevity by cell non-autonomously reprogramming organismal lipid metabolism and activating autophagy. Glial XBP-1s regulates the activation of another transcription factor, HLH-30/TFEB, in the intestine. HLH-30 activates intestinal autophagy, increases intestinal lipid catabolism, and upregulates a robust transcriptional program. Our study reveals a novel role for glia in regulating peripheral lipid metabolism, autophagy, and organellar health through peripheral activation of HLH-30 and autophagy”","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-10T06:26:16.755Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tww9s66w69","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tww9s66w69"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Melissa","last_name":"Metcalf","orcid":"0000-0003-0515-5784"},{"first_name":"Jenni","last_name":"Durieux","orcid":"0000-0002-0016-7332"},{"first_name":"Larry","last_name":"Joe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Dillin","orcid":"0000-0002-7427-2629"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0187kwz08","name":"National Institute for Health Research","grant_id":"R01AG059566"}],"categories":["Glia","Aging","Transcription Factor","Autophagy","Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":120410,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":153,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":120410,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":120104,"restricted":58,"embargoed":248},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2154},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":595},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":460},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":415},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":405},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":390},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":352},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":315},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":274},{"id":